      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Einstein called it \"spooky action at a distance\" because it seemed to violate the cosmic speed limit—the speed of light—by suggesting an instantaneous connection across vast distances. The weirdness, the truly *spooky* part, is that before you measure either particle, neither has a definite property; they are both in a superposition of possibilities. It's not like having two pre-set gloves in separate boxes; if you open one box and find the left glove, you *know* the other is right, but the gloves *were* always left and right. With entangled particles, they are both \"left *and* right\" simultaneously until the act of observation forces both into a definite, correlated state instantly.",
